@h1 Writing text h1@

Type sentences. Separate paragraphs with a blank line. That is a complete Wit
document — everything on this page is what to add when plain prose isn't enough.

@h2 Paragraphs h2@

A blank line starts a new paragraph. A single newline inside a block is a soft
wrap: HTML collapses it to a space, so the sentence flows on.

@@@pre
The reactor logged a coolant spike at 03:12.

Two operators were on shift; neither
had left the control room.
pre@@@

That renders two paragraphs — the first line, then the two soft-wrapped lines as a
single flowing paragraph. One blank line or several makes no difference, and
extra blank lines never produce empty paragraphs. A "blank" line counts even if
it holds only spaces or tabs, so text pasted from an editor that leaves trailing
whitespace still splits correctly.

Leading indentation, line length, and line endings (CRLF, CR, LF) carry no
meaning — they are normalised before paragraphs are split. Wit has no
Markdown-style block syntax, so a line that opens with @code - code@,
@code * code@, @code > code@, or @code 1. code@ is literal prose, not a list or
quote. Punctuation is never reserved: em-dashes, apostrophes, ampersands, and
percent signs render as themselves, and quotes stay straight (no smart-quote
substitution).

@h2 Bold and italic h2@

Two inline marks: @code *asterisks* code@ for *bold*, @code _underscores_ code@
for _italic_. Each wraps a run of text and can span several words.

@@@pre
The lens held _the last of the evening light_, and the flame *never wavered*.
pre@@@

A mark only fires when the character flanks the text from the outside. Pressed
against a digit or a letter it stays literal, which is why arithmetic and
identifiers are safe with emphasis left on:

@@@pre
The product 5*6*7 and the path file_name_here both stay plain text.
pre@@@

Nest them with *bold on the outside*: @@@code *_both_* code@@@ gives
bold-and-italic. The reverse, @@@code _*x*_ code@@@, leaves the inner asterisks
literal — the parser resolves the outer mark first, and italic-outside consumes
its span before the inner bold is seen.

@aside(class note) 💡 A mark refuses to touch a token containing its own
character — the underscore mark will not italicise @code snake_case code@. For
that, use the node form: @@@code @em snake_case em@ code@@@ (italic) or
@@@code @strong … strong@ code@@@ (bold). An empty pair like @code __ code@ also
stays literal. aside@

@h2 Inline mark nodes h2@

For anything past bold and italic, call the mark by name. Each is an inline node
mapping to the same-named HTML element:

@@@pre
@code … code@    inline code: commands, identifiers, paths
@u … u@       underline (reads as a link online — use sparingly)
@s … s@       strike-through: retractions, "was X, now Y"
@sub … sub@     subscript: chemical formulae, footnote marks
@sup … sup@     superscript: exponents, ordinals, note refs
@mark … mark@    highlight: draw the eye to a phrase
@small … small@   small print, fine print
@br         a hard line break inside one paragraph
pre@@@

In a single run:

@@@pre
Call @code shutdown() code@ to scram; water is H@sub 2 sub@O, the area is
4@sup 2 sup@, an @s old figure s@ became a @mark new one mark@, with
@small caveats small@ in the footnotes.
pre@@@

@@@code @br code@@@ is self-closing and needs no body. A lone newline is a soft
break; use @@@code @br code@@@ for a real one — an address block, a line of verse,
a signature:

@@@pre
Ada Lovelace @br
12 Analytical Way @br
London
pre@@@

Markdown output has no reliable inline break, so @@@code @br code@@@ degrades to a
space there. Keep to HTML or PDF when hard breaks matter.

@h2 Comments h2@

A comment is a note that never reaches the reader — stripped before any output.
A tilde followed by a space starts a line comment to end-of-line:

@@@pre
~ verify the coolant spike time against the incident log

The reactor logged a spike at 03:12.
pre@@@

Two tildes open a block comment; the fence @@@code ~~/ code@@@ closes it. It can
vanish mid-sentence or span many lines:

@@@pre
The bell ~~ TODO: confirm the year ~~/ rang on the hour.

~~
Draft note: this passage may move to chapter two.
Ask the editor before cutting it.
~~/
pre@@@

Only the exact closing fence ends a block, so a home path like
@code ~/Documents code@ or a lone doubled tilde inside the block is safe content.

@aside(class note) 💡 Put a blank line on each side of a standalone comment.
A line comment wedged between two prose lines eats the newlines around it and
runs the paragraphs together; a comment as the first line of a container body can
fold the next block into a paragraph. A blank line each side prevents both. aside@

@h2 Escaping edge characters h2@

Six characters lead a structural reader: @code @ code@, @code # code@,
@@@code | code@@@, @@@code * code@@@, @@@code _ code@@@, and @code ~ code@. A
backslash keeps any of them literal — the backslash is dropped, the character
stays.

@@@pre
Email me at ada\@example.org today.
Write \#tag for a literal hash, C\# for the key.
The range is 4\|8 amps, a literal \*asterisk\*, and \~ leads a line.
pre@@@

The escape is only needed when the character is glued to a letter (or leads a
line, for @code # code@ and @code ~ code@). A character before a space or a digit
is already safe: "Meet me @ home", "in @2024", "it took ~6 hours", and
"x ~ y" all render as typed, no backslash required. Most prose needs no escapes
at all.

@aside(class note) 💡 An email won't survive inside an inline
@@@code @code … code@ code@@@ node — the @code @ code@ reads as the node's close.
Escape it in prose (@@@code ada\@example.org code@@@), or open a frozen code
node with @@@code @@@code code@@@ and close it with the matching triple-fence: it
renders its body verbatim, at-signs and all. aside@

